REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w02","title":"Parts of speech and articles","summary":"The kinds of words that build an English sentence (verb, noun, pronoun, adjective, adverb, preposition, article, conjunction, interjection): what job each one does and how to spot it. Then the articles a, an and the, and the places where English uses no article at all.","n":2,"lectures":[{"code":"2.1","title":"Parts of speech(verb, noun & pronoun)","video":"RvcNFUfeqHI","seconds":2213},{"code":"2.2","title":"Parts of speech(adjectives & adverbs)","video":"1xo6TWVjYbE","seconds":2050},{"code":"2.3","title":"Parts of speech (prepositions)","video":"ajGNF1CO_Vs","seconds":1538},{"code":"2.4","title":"Parts of speech(conjunctions & interjections)","video":"uxCVjgMfW34","seconds":1403},{"code":"2.5","title":"Articles- part 1 | definite & indefinite articles in english usage","video":"KXWalyM03b8","seconds":1282},{"code":"2.6","title":"Articles- part 2 | usage of the definite article and the zero article","video":"V-1ZDPS6EC0","seconds":940}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"parts-of-speech","level":2,"title":"Parts of speech"},{"id":"verbs","level":2,"title":"Verbs"},{"id":"nouns","level":2,"title":"Nouns"},{"id":"pronouns","level":2,"title":"Pronouns"},{"id":"adjectives","level":2,"title":"Adjectives"},{"id":"before-the-noun-or-after-the-verb","level":3,"title":"Before the noun or after the verb"},{"id":"adverbs","level":2,"title":"Adverbs"},{"id":"subject-and-object","level":2,"title":"Subject and object"},{"id":"prepositions","level":2,"title":"Prepositions"},{"id":"simple-and-compound-prepositions","level":3,"title":"Simple and compound prepositions"},{"id":"time-place-direction-and-agent","level":3,"title":"Time, place, direction and agent"},{"id":"thirty-two-common-prepositions","level":3,"title":"Thirty-two common prepositions"},{"id":"conjunctions","level":2,"title":"Conjunctions"},{"id":"coordinating-conjunctions","level":3,"title":"Coordinating conjunctions"},{"id":"subordinating-conjunctions","level":3,"title":"Subordinating conjunctions"},{"id":"correlative-conjunctions","level":3,"title":"Correlative conjunctions"},{"id":"interjections","level":2,"title":"Interjections"},{"id":"articles-a-and-an","level":2,"title":"Articles: a and an"},{"id":"five-uses-of-a-and-an","level":3,"title":"Five uses of a and an"},{"id":"the-definite-article","level":2,"title":"The definite article"},{"id":"the-zero-article","level":2,"title":"The zero article"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>part of speech</strong> is the name of a word by the <strong>job it does</strong> in a sentence. The course lists eight: <strong>noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, article, conjunction</strong>. <strong>Interjections</strong> come in Lecture 2.4.</li>\n<li>A <strong>verb</strong> shows an <strong>action</strong> (<em>plays</em>) or a <strong>state</strong> (<em>am</em>). Every sentence must have a verb, and only verbs carry <strong>tense</strong> (past, present, future). <strong>Auxiliary (helping) verbs</strong> (<em>am, is, can, must …</em>) help the <strong>main verb</strong>. The verb <strong>agrees</strong> with its subject: <em>he plays</em>, <em>they play</em>, <em>you are</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>noun</strong> is a naming word: <strong>countable</strong> (proper, class, collective) or <strong>uncountable</strong> (abstract, material). Only nouns take a plural, and an article or a preposition is always followed by a noun. A <strong>pronoun</strong> replaces a noun: <em>I, he, they</em> (subject), <em>me, him, them</em> (object).</li>\n<li>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or a pronoun, before it (<em>a big car</em>) or after the verb (<em>this car is big</em>). Order: <strong>opinion, size, colour, type</strong>. An <strong>adverb</strong> tells about a verb, an adjective or another adverb, and answers <strong>when, where, how, why</strong>. Not every adverb ends in <em>-ly</em>. The <strong>subject</strong> is the doer (who?); the <strong>object</strong> receives the action (what?).</li>\n<li>A <strong>preposition</strong> shows a relation of <strong>time, place or position</strong> and comes <strong>before</strong> a noun: <em>at 4.00 am, on Monday, in 2017, for three days</em>. Indian languages put such words <strong>after</strong> the noun (<strong>postpositions</strong>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>conjunction</strong> joins words, phrases or clauses: <strong>coordinating</strong> (FANBOYS, equal parts), <strong>subordinating</strong> (joins a dependent clause to a main clause: <em>because, although, if</em>), <strong>correlative</strong> (pairs: <em>either … or</em>). An <strong>interjection</strong> (<em>wow, ouch, oops</em>) shows a sudden emotion and stands apart from the sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Articles</strong>: <em>a</em> before a consonant <strong>sound</strong>, <em>an</em> before a vowel <strong>sound</strong> (<em>an hour, a European trip</em>). <em>The</em> points to a known, unique or specified thing (<em>the sun, the Ganga, the brightest girl</em>). <strong>Zero article</strong>: no article for daily-routine places (<em>go home</em>) and for material and abstract nouns used in general (<em>gold, care</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"parts-of-speech\">Parts of speech</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Language is built in layers. <strong>Sounds</strong> join to make <strong>words</strong>, and words join to make a <strong>sentence</strong>, the basic unit of what we say (<em>Riyad plays cricket</em>). Inside a sentence each word does a job: one names a person, one tells the action, one describes. We sort words by that job, and each group is called a <strong>part of speech</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Part of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>part of speech</strong> is “the name given to a word based on the job it does in a sentence”. There are <strong>eight</strong> parts of speech. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of speech</th>\n<th>Its job</th>\n<th>Example from the lectures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Noun</td>\n<td>names a person, place, thing, animal, feeling or idea</td>\n<td><em><strong>Arjun</strong></em> went to <em><strong>Greece</strong></em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pronoun</td>\n<td>replaces a noun</td>\n<td><em><strong>He</strong></em> enjoyed spending time with <em><strong>his</strong></em> mother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verb</td>\n<td>shows an action or a state</td>\n<td>Riyad <em><strong>plays</strong></em> cricket; I <em><strong>am</strong></em> a teacher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjective</td>\n<td>describes a noun or a pronoun</td>\n<td>a <em><strong>big</strong></em> car</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adverb</td>\n<td>tells about a verb, an adjective or an adverb</td>\n<td>we walked <em><strong>slowly</strong></em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preposition</td>\n<td>shows time, place or position, before a noun</td>\n<td><em><strong>on</strong></em> Monday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article</td>\n<td><em>a, an, the</em>, before a noun</td>\n<td><em><strong>a</strong></em> cat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjunction</td>\n<td>joins words, phrases or clauses</td>\n<td>Jack <em><strong>and</strong></em> Jill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Eight, and one more</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide in Lecture 2.1 lists <strong>article</strong> as the eighth part of speech. Many grammar books list <strong>interjection</strong> (<em>wow, ouch</em>) as the eighth instead. This course teaches both: see <a href=\"#/english1/w02/interjections\">Interjections</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Words this page uses</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: who or what the sentence is about, usually the doer (<em><strong>Riyad</strong> plays cricket</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Singular</strong> means one (<em>cat</em>); <strong>plural</strong> means more than one (<em>cats</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong>: the time of an action: past, present or future.</li>\n<li><strong>Phrase</strong>: a group of words without its own subject and verb (<em>in a beautiful garden</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clause</strong>: a group of words with its own subject and verb (<em>he is my friend</em>). A simple sentence is one clause.</li>\n<li><strong>Modify</strong>: to add information to another word, to describe it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learn the eight names well</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer: these eight terms “are going to appear several times in the course”, and the more clearly you know them, the better you perform. Take sentences from anything you read and find each part of speech in them. It is worth half an hour on each category. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"verbs\">Verbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A verb tells <strong>what happens</strong> (an action: <em>play, fight, dance</em>) or <strong>what is</strong> (a state: <em>am, is</em>). Take the verb out of <em>Riyad plays cricket</em> and you get <em>Riyad cricket</em>: just a string of words. That is why the lecture starts with verbs: the verb is the most important element of a sentence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>verb</strong> is an <strong>action</strong> word or a <strong>state</strong> word. The slide gives three facts: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Every sentence in English <strong>must have a verb</strong>. Without a verb, a group of words is a phrase or just a sequence of words, not a sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Only verbs have tenses</strong> (past, present, future). We learn the time of a sentence from its verb.</li>\n<li>A verb shows an <strong>action</strong> or a <strong>state</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s two sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Riyad <strong>plays</strong> cricket three times a week.</em> The verb <em>plays</em> is a <strong>physical action</strong>, and it carries the present tense. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><em>I <strong>am</strong> a teacher.</em> There is no action here. The verb <em>am</em> shows a <strong>state</strong> (a situation). <em>Am</em> is an <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, and it too marks the present tense. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide’s tense rules for the present. <strong>V1</strong> means the first form (base form) of the verb, the form in the dictionary: <em>play, read, sing</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Action verb</th>\n<th>The verb <em>be</em> (and <em>have</em>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>V1: <em>I play</em></td>\n<td><em>I am</em> (<em>have</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He / She / It (singular)</td>\n<td>V1 + s: <em>he plays</em></td>\n<td><em>he is</em> (<em>has</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You / We / They</td>\n<td>V1: <em>they play</em></td>\n<td><em>they are</em> (<em>have</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Only <em>I</em> takes <em>am</em>: the lecturer says <em>I</em> has a special status, so it has its own marker. The slide also lists <em>had</em>, the past form of <em>have</em> for every subject.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Auxiliary verb (helping verb)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, or <strong>helping verb</strong>, “is a verb that changes or helps another verb”. <em>Auxiliary</em> means “extra”. Auxiliaries such as <em>do</em> can make a question (for example <em>Do you play?</em>). They are usually used with other verbs. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n<p>The slide’s list:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>be</strong>: <em>am, are, is</em> (present), <em>was, were</em> (past), <em>being</em></li>\n<li><strong>do</strong>: <em>did, does, doing</em>; <strong>have</strong>: <em>had, has, having</em></li>\n<li><em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Main verb (action verb)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>main verb</strong> carries the action: <em>play, keep, take, dance, get</em>. Every language has countless action verbs, but its auxiliary verbs fit on a short list. Action verbs are of two types, <strong>transitive</strong> and <strong>intransitive</strong>; the lecture names them here and returns to them later. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Transitive and intransitive, in one line</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>transitive</strong> verb takes an object: <em>Riyad plays <strong>cricket</strong></em> (plays what? cricket). An <strong>intransitive</strong> verb needs no object: <em>She danced so well.</em> (This line is an explanation added here; the lecture only names the two types.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the verbs (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>They <strong>fought</strong> like cats and dogs.</td>\n<td>fought</td>\n<td>action (past of <em>fight</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Please <strong>keep</strong> the pen on the book.</td>\n<td>keep</td>\n<td>action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Philosophy <strong>is</strong> an interesting subject.</td>\n<td>is</td>\n<td>state (auxiliary <em>be</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>took</strong> a beautiful photo of the sunrise.</td>\n<td>took</td>\n<td>action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>love</strong> you.</td>\n<td>love</td>\n<td>a feeling (state)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She <strong>danced</strong> so well.</td>\n<td>danced</td>\n<td>action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It <strong>is</strong> a beautiful dance.</td>\n<td>is</td>\n<td>state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Go!</strong></td>\n<td>go</td>\n<td>action: a one-word sentence still has a verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It <strong>has</strong> eight legs.</td>\n<td>has</td>\n<td>state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Can</strong> you <strong>get</strong> a glass of water?</td>\n<td>can, get</td>\n<td>auxiliary + main verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You <strong>must get</strong> them.</td>\n<td>must, get</td>\n<td>auxiliary + main verb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> every sentence has a verb. Some have only an auxiliary (<em>is</em>), some only a main verb (<em>keep</em>), some both (<em>must get</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>Can</em> and <em>must</em> help the main verb <em>get</em>; a verb that assists a main verb like this is an auxiliary, a helping verb. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Only one verb agrees with the subject</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a sentence, <strong>only one verb works with the subject</strong>: it changes for singular and plural (<em>he plays</em>, <em>they play</em>). This matching is called <strong>agreement</strong> (subject–verb agreement). Other words that look like verbs are <strong>verbal nouns</strong>: nouns made from verbs. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s example: <em>Your dogs <strong>like</strong> to go for a walk.</em> It seems to have three verbs: <em>like, go, walk</em>. But only <em>like</em> changes with the subject <em>dogs</em> (plural, so <em>like</em>, not <em>likes</em>). <em>To go for a walk</em> answers “like <strong>what</strong>?”: it is a verbal noun, really a noun made from a verb. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">One such verb in each clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The rule is about one clause. When a conjunction joins two clauses (Lecture 2.4), each clause has its own verb: <em>It <strong>was</strong> raining, so we <strong>did</strong> not go out.</em> (Note added here.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Choose the verb that makes the sentence grammatical (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>My uncle (is / are) making dinner.</td>\n<td><strong>is</strong></td>\n<td><em>uncle</em> is singular; <em>is</em> is the auxiliary, <em>making</em> the main verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Your friends (is / are) nice.</td>\n<td><strong>are</strong></td>\n<td><em>friends</em> is plural; <em>are</em> is the only verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The teacher (reads / read) every day to the children.</td>\n<td><strong>reads</strong></td>\n<td><em>teacher</em> is singular: V1 + s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Your dogs (likes / like) to go for a walk.</td>\n<td><strong>like</strong></td>\n<td><em>dogs</em> is plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They (is / are) swimming in the river.</td>\n<td><strong>are</strong></td>\n<td><em>they</em> is plural; <em>are</em> carries the tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You (is / are) making a lot of noise.</td>\n<td><strong>are</strong></td>\n<td><em>you</em> always takes the plural form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She (sings / sing) very well.</td>\n<td><strong>sings</strong></td>\n<td><em>she</em> is singular: V1 + s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The (child / children) plays in the park.</td>\n<td><strong>child</strong></td>\n<td><em>plays</em> (V1 + s) needs a singular subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The (children / child) play in the park.</td>\n<td><strong>children</strong></td>\n<td><em>play</em> (V1) needs a plural subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They (have / has) a bottle.</td>\n<td><strong>have</strong></td>\n<td><em>they</em> is plural</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>You</em> is always plural in grammar</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>You</em> is the <strong>second-person</strong> pronoun: the person we speak to. Even when it means one person, it takes the plural form: <em>you <strong>are</strong> making a lot of noise</em>, never ✗ <em>you is</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"nouns\">Nouns</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Everything we can talk about has a name: a person (<em>Arjun</em>), a place (<em>Greece</em>), a thing (<em>pen</em>), an animal (<em>cat</em>), a feeling (<em>love</em>), an idea (<em>success</em>). These names are <strong>nouns</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>noun</strong> is a <strong>naming word</strong>: the name given to a person, place, thing, animal, feeling or idea.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Animals: <em>dogs, cats, birds, hen</em>. Things: <em>chair, computer, pen</em>.</li>\n<li>Feelings: <em>love, anger</em>. Ideas: <em>success, discipline</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentence, with the nouns in bold: <em><strong>Arjun</strong></em> went to <em><strong>Greece</strong></em> for <em><strong>vacation</strong></em> with his <em><strong>family</strong></em> last <em><strong>year</strong></em>. (<em>Went</em> is the verb.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></p>\n<p>Nouns are first sorted into <strong>countable</strong> and <strong>uncountable</strong> nouns, then into five types. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of noun</th>\n<th>What it names, with the slide’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Proper</strong> noun (countable)</td>\n<td>only one object: <em>John, Patna, Sunday, India</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Class</strong> noun (countable)</td>\n<td>the whole class: <em>cat, tiger, house, pine, hill</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Collective</strong> noun (countable)</td>\n<td>a whole class as one group, a kind of class noun: <em>cabinet, police, jury, government, corporation</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Abstract</strong> noun (uncountable)</td>\n<td>an idea: <em>ability, gravity, goodness, hardship</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Material</strong> noun (uncountable)</td>\n<td>a concrete object, a substance: <em>gold, water, rice, wheat</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Class noun or proper noun?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Cat</em> is not one particular cat: it is the name for the whole class of cats, so it is a <strong>class noun</strong> (many books call it a “common noun”). <em>India</em> names one particular country, so it is a <strong>proper noun</strong>, and it starts with a capital letter. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gold and gravity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both are <strong>uncountable</strong>: we do not say ✗ <em>three golds</em> or ✗ <em>two gravities</em>. But <em>gold</em> is a <strong>material</strong> noun (a substance you can touch), while <em>gravity</em> is an <strong>abstract</strong> noun (an idea). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the nouns (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>They fought like <strong>cats</strong> and <strong>dogs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Please keep the <strong>pen</strong> on the <strong>book</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Philosophy</strong> is an interesting <strong>subject</strong>.</li>\n<li>I took a beautiful <strong>photo</strong> of the <strong>sunrise</strong>.</li>\n<li>I love you. No noun here: <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> are pronouns.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three signs of a noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Only nouns can be made plural</strong>: <em>cat → cats, dog → dogs, pen → pens, book → books</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a></li>\n<li>An <strong>article</strong> (<em>a, an, the</em>) comes only in front of a noun. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a></li>\n<li>A <strong>preposition</strong> (<em>on, in, at …</em>) comes only in front of a noun (or a pronoun).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So in <em>on the book</em>, <em>on</em> is a preposition, <em>the</em> is an article, and <em>book</em> must be a noun. An adjective may stand in between (<em>a <strong>beautiful</strong> photo</em>), but a noun always follows.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"pronouns\">Pronouns</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read this: <em>Rahul went to Kerala with Rahul’s mother. Rahul really enjoyed spending time with Rahul’s mother.</em> It sounds odd, as if there were two people called Rahul. Small words that stand in for the noun fix it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pronoun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>pronoun</strong> is a word that <strong>replaces a noun</strong>: it comes in the position of a noun. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rewrite with pronouns (the lecture’s example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Start: <em>Rahul went to Kerala with Rahul’s mother. Rahul really enjoyed spending time with Rahul’s mother.</em></li>\n<li>Keep the first <em>Rahul</em>, so the reader knows who it is.</li>\n<li>Replace <em>Rahul’s</em> with <strong>his</strong>: <em>Rahul went to Kerala with <strong>his</strong> mother.</em></li>\n<li>In the second sentence, replace <em>Rahul</em> with <strong>He</strong> and <em>Rahul’s</em> with <strong>his</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Rahul went to Kerala with his mother. He really enjoyed spending time with his mother.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject pronoun (the doer)</th>\n<th>Object pronoun (receives the action)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>me</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>you</td>\n<td>you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>he</td>\n<td>him</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>she</td>\n<td>her</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it</td>\n<td>it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>we</td>\n<td>us</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>they</td>\n<td>them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>English has other pronouns too, such as <em>his, hers, this, that</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the pronouns (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvcNFUfeqHI&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>They</strong> fought like cats and dogs. (<em>They</em> refers to some people outside this sentence.)</li>\n<li><strong>You</strong> please keep the pen on top of the book.</li>\n<li>Philosophy? Yes. <strong>That</strong> is an interesting subject.</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong> took a beautiful photo of the sunrise.</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong> love <strong>you</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>She</strong> danced so well.</li>\n<li><strong>It</strong> is a beautiful dance.</li>\n<li>Go! (no pronoun)</li>\n<li><strong>It</strong> has eight legs.</li>\n<li>Can <strong>you</strong> get a glass of water, please?</li>\n<li><strong>You</strong> must get <strong>them</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"adjectives\">Adjectives</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A noun names a thing; an <strong>adjective</strong> adds detail about it. <em>She drives a car</em> is a full sentence. <em>She drives an amazing, big, red, sports car</em> tells us much more about the car. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjective</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes, or gives information about, a <strong>noun</strong> or a <strong>pronoun</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Nouns and pronouns are two different parts of speech, but a pronoun stands for a noun. So an adjective can describe a pronoun just as it describes a noun: <em>He was <strong>thin</strong></em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Many adjectives, one noun</strong>: <em>She drives an <strong>amazing, big, red, sports</strong> car.</em> Four adjectives modify the one noun <em>car</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>One adjective, many nouns</strong>: <em>She has <strong>amazing</strong> cars, bikes and computers.</em> One adjective modifies three nouns. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Order of adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When several adjectives come before one noun, they go in this order: <strong>opinion → size → colour → type</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Answer (the adjective)</th>\n<th>Information</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What is your opinion about the car?</td>\n<td>amazing</td>\n<td>opinion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What size is the car?</td>\n<td>big</td>\n<td>size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What colour is the car?</td>\n<td>red</td>\n<td>colour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What type of car?</td>\n<td>sports</td>\n<td>type</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<p>These four are only a few examples of the information an adjective can give. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"before-the-noun-or-after-the-verb\">Before the noun or after the verb</h3>\n<p>Adjectives can also come after the noun: <em>This car is big. This car is amazing. This car is red.</em> The job is the same (they still describe <em>car</em>); only the position changes. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attributive and predicative adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An adjective <strong>before</strong> the noun: <em>an <strong>amazing</strong> car</em>. The lecture calls these <strong>distributive</strong> adjectives; most grammar books call them <strong>attributive</strong> adjectives. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n<li>An adjective <strong>after</strong> the verb that carries the tense (<em>is, was</em>): <em>This car is <strong>big</strong></em>. This is a <strong>predicative</strong> adjective, because it stands in the <strong>predicate</strong>: the part of the sentence after the subject (the verb and everything after it).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two meanings of “distributive”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In many grammar books, <strong>distributive adjectives</strong> are <em>each, every, either, neither</em> (<em>each student</em>). For “an adjective before the noun”, the safer name is <strong>attributive</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the adjectives (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Adjective → the word it describes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Worst fellow!</td>\n<td><strong>worst</strong> → fellow (a sarcastic or joking remark)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beautiful sunrise!</td>\n<td><strong>beautiful</strong> → sunrise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It was a majestic building with narrow corridors.</td>\n<td><strong>majestic</strong> → building; <strong>narrow</strong> → corridors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>That is a huge, black, African elephant.</td>\n<td><strong>huge, black, African</strong> → elephant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She is a very sweet girl.</td>\n<td><strong>sweet</strong> → girl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>That is a fragrant sweet.</td>\n<td><strong>fragrant</strong> → sweet (here <em>sweet</em> is a noun: a sweet dish)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He is a tall, handsome boy.</td>\n<td><strong>tall, handsome</strong> → boy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I came from Delhi.</td>\n<td>none: <em>from Delhi</em> tells <strong>where</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He sings well.</td>\n<td>none: <em>well</em> tells <strong>how</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarah has a beautiful yellow frock.</td>\n<td><strong>beautiful, yellow</strong> → frock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He was thin, pale and yellow.</td>\n<td><strong>thin, pale, yellow</strong> → he (predicative)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">An adjective describes the noun after it</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>a majestic building with narrow corridors</em>, <em>narrow</em> describes <em>corridors</em>, the noun after it, not <em>building</em> before it. The lecture: “the scope of an adjective is limited”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Same word, different part of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Sweet</em> is an <strong>adjective</strong> in <em>a very sweet girl</em> (it describes <em>girl</em>). It is a <strong>noun</strong> in <em>a fragrant sweet</em> (it is the thing described, after the article <em>a</em> and the adjective <em>fragrant</em>). Decide by the job the word does in that sentence.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"adverbs\">Adverbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The name says it: an <strong>adverb</strong> <strong>adds</strong> to the <strong>verb</strong>. It tells more about the action: <strong>when</strong> it happened, <strong>where</strong>, <strong>how</strong>, <strong>why</strong>, <strong>in what way</strong>. In <em>He sings well</em>, <em>well</em> tells how he sings. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Adverbs</strong> are words that give information about <strong>verbs</strong>, <strong>adjectives</strong> and other <strong>adverbs</strong>. They usually answer the questions <strong>when, why, how, in what way</strong>, and so on. An adverb can be one word (<em>slowly</em>) or a group of words (<em>in a very beautiful garden</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Take one sentence apart (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Yesterday evening, we walked somewhat slowly in a very beautiful garden.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the verb: <strong>walked</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>How</em> did we walk? <strong>Slowly</strong>. So <em>slowly</em> is an adverb of the verb <em>walked</em>.</li>\n<li><em>How</em> slowly? <strong>Somewhat</strong> slowly. So <em>somewhat</em> is an adverb that modifies another adverb, <em>slowly</em>.</li>\n<li><em>What kind of</em> garden? <strong>Beautiful</strong>: an adjective of the noun <em>garden</em>.</li>\n<li><em>How</em> beautiful? <strong>Very</strong> beautiful. So <em>very</em> is an adverb that modifies an adjective.</li>\n<li><em>When</em> did we walk? <strong>Yesterday evening</strong>. Inside it, <em>yesterday</em> describes the noun <em>evening</em>, but the whole phrase answers <em>when?</em>, so the phrase works as an adverb of <em>walked</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Where</em> did we walk? <strong>In a very beautiful garden</strong>. The whole phrase answers <em>where?</em>, so it also works as an adverb of <em>walked</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> adverbs: <em>slowly</em> (→ walked), <em>somewhat</em> (→ slowly), <em>very</em> (→ beautiful). Adverb phrases: <em>yesterday evening</em> (when), <em>in a very beautiful garden</em> (where).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So the two “not adjectives” from the adjective exercise are adverbs: <em>He sings <strong>well</strong></em> (how?) and <em>I came <strong>from Delhi</strong></em> (a phrase that tells the place: where?). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the adverbs (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Adverb → the word it modifies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>She sings well.</td>\n<td><strong>well</strong> → sings (how?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The coffee is very hot.</td>\n<td><strong>very</strong> → hot (an adjective)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vani is an extremely good singer.</td>\n<td><strong>extremely</strong> → good (an adjective); together, <em>extremely good</em> still describes <em>singer</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is too hot today.</td>\n<td><strong>too</strong> → hot (an adjective); <em>today</em> also answers <em>when?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She speaks foreign languages fluently.</td>\n<td><strong>fluently</strong> → speaks (in what way?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The kid is now happy.</td>\n<td><strong>now</strong> → happy (when?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He rides his vehicle very carefully.</td>\n<td><strong>carefully</strong> → rides; <strong>very</strong> → carefully</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I visit my friend daily.</td>\n<td><strong>daily</strong> → visit (how often?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He will return immediately.</td>\n<td><strong>immediately</strong> → return (when?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She runs very fast.</td>\n<td><strong>fast</strong> → runs; <strong>very</strong> → fast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run quickly.</td>\n<td><strong>quickly</strong> → run</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not every adverb ends in -ly</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many adverbs end in <strong>-ly</strong> (<em>quickly, immediately, carefully, fluently</em>), and simple grammar books sometimes say “words ending in -ly are adverbs”. That is only partly true: <em>well, fast, very, too, now, somewhat</em> are adverbs without <em>-ly</em>. Look at the <strong>function</strong>: which question does the word answer, and which word does it modify? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The question test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To find an adverb, ask a question about the verb (or about the adjective): <strong>How? When? Where? Why? How often? How much?</strong> The word or phrase that answers is an adverb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"subject-and-object\">Subject and object</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Most sentences tell us <strong>who</strong> did something and <strong>what</strong> it was done to. In <em>Ram killed Raavan</em>, Ram is the doer and Raavan receives the action. These two roles are the <strong>subject</strong> and the <strong>object</strong>. They are <strong>grammatical relations</strong>: the relation of one word to another word in the sentence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Subject</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>subject</strong> of a sentence is usually a <strong>noun</strong> or a <strong>pronoun</strong>. It answers the question <strong>who?</strong>: it is the <strong>doer</strong> of the action. (A longer phrase can be a subject too; it then does the job of a noun.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Predicate</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Everything in a sentence other than the subject is the <strong>predicate</strong>: the verb and all that follows it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>object</strong> of a sentence is usually a <strong>noun</strong> or a <strong>pronoun</strong>. It is a word, or a group of words, that <strong>receives the action</strong> of the subject. It answers the question <strong>what?</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Subject (who?)</th>\n<th>Object (what?)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Riyad plays cricket every Sunday.</td>\n<td>Riyad (noun)</td>\n<td>cricket (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ram killed Raavan.</td>\n<td>Ram (noun)</td>\n<td>Raavan (noun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Riyad did not like her.</td>\n<td>Riyad (noun)</td>\n<td>her (pronoun)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They are nice people.</td>\n<td>They (pronoun)</td>\n<td>the slide marks <em>people</em> (see the note)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>They are nice people</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide marks <em>people</em> along with the objects. Strictly, a noun after <em>am, is, are</em> receives no action: it renames the subject (<em>they</em> = <em>nice people</em>), and grammar books call it a <strong>complement</strong>. The point to keep from the lecture: subjects and objects are nouns or pronouns.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the subject and the object (the lecture’s exercise at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xo6TWVjYbE&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>I want a glass of water.</em> Subject: <strong>I</strong>. Verb: <em>want</em>. Object: <strong>a glass of water</strong>, the whole group (<em>glass</em> and <em>water</em> are both nouns).</li>\n<li><em>He loves his family dearly.</em> Subject: <strong>He</strong>. Object: <strong>his family</strong>. <em>Dearly</em> is an adverb (how?).</li>\n<li><em>They ruined the house.</em> Subject: <strong>They</strong>. Object: <strong>the house</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"prepositions\">Prepositions</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Small words like <em>at, on, in, before</em> tell us <strong>where</strong> or <strong>when</strong> something is, compared with something else: <em>at the office, on Monday, before me, in the queue</em>. They are called <strong>pre-positions</strong> because their position is <strong>before</strong> a noun. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Closed class and open class</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>closed class</strong> of words has a <strong>limited</strong> number of members: <strong>pronouns, prepositions, articles</strong>. These words are valued for their grammatical job, so they are also called <strong>function words</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></li>\n<li>An <strong>open class</strong> has no limit: there are millions of <strong>nouns</strong>, and very many <strong>verbs</strong> and <strong>adjectives</strong>, and new ones keep coming. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Preposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Prepositions</strong> are words that help us show <strong>relationships in time, place and position</strong> between the noun that follows them and the rest of the sentence. Examples: <em>in, on, at, by, from, with, before, after, for</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s two sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I will see you <strong>at</strong> the office <strong>on</strong> Monday.</em> <em>At</em> is a preposition of <strong>place</strong>; <em>on</em> is a preposition of <strong>time</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>He stood <strong>before</strong> me <strong>in</strong> the queue.</em> <em>Before</em> is a preposition of <strong>position</strong>; <em>in</em> is a preposition of <strong>place</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What follows a preposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>What follows a preposition can only be a <strong>noun</strong> (or a noun phrase) or a <strong>pronoun</strong>, because a pronoun replaces a noun. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>on <strong>Monday</strong></em>: a noun.</li>\n<li><em>at <strong>the office</strong></em>: a <strong>noun phrase</strong>. <em>The</em> is an article, also called a <strong>determiner</strong>, and it too comes only before a noun. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n<li><em>before <strong>me</strong></em>: a pronoun.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Prepositional phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A preposition and the noun (phrase) after it make a <strong>prepositional phrase</strong>: <em>on Monday, at the office, in the queue</em>. In speech and in writing, sentences usually grow longer by adding more prepositional phrases. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Postpositions in Indian languages</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Hindi, Marathi, Bengali and other Indian languages, words with this job come <strong>after</strong> the noun, so they are called <strong>postpositions</strong>. For example, Hindi <em>ghar mein</em> is word for word “house in”, that is, <em>in the house</em> (example added here). The lecturer does not recommend learning through translation, but he suggests saying the two example sentences in your own language and noticing where these words go. Comparing the two makes learning easier and faster. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"simple-and-compound-prepositions\">Simple and compound prepositions</h3>\n<p>The lecture shows a long alphabetical list of prepositions (from eslforums.com). It is a closed class: long, but not endless. Most are one word (<em>about, above, at, by, in, on, with</em>). Some have two or more words but do exactly the same job: these are <strong>compound</strong> (complex) prepositions. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>according to, ahead of, because of, by means of, due to, instead of, next to, out of</em></li>\n<li><em>as far as, as well as, in accordance with, in addition to, in case of, in front of, in lieu of, in place of, in spite of, with a view to</em></li>\n<li><em>into</em> (<em>in</em> + <em>to</em>) and <em>within</em> (<em>with</em> + <em>in</em>) are written as one word.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"time-place-direction-and-agent\">Time, place, direction and agent</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s tables: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Preposition</th>\n<th>Used for, with the slide’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>at</strong> (time)</td>\n<td>a specific, precise time: <em>at 4.00 am; at noon</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>on</strong> (time)</td>\n<td>days and dates: <em>on Wednesday; on 25th June</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>in</strong> (time)</td>\n<td>longer periods, such as months, years, seasons: <em>in January; in 2017; in summer</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>for</strong> (time)</td>\n<td>a duration of time: <em>for three days; for a year</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>at</strong> (place)</td>\n<td>a specific place: <em>at the library</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>on</strong> (place)</td>\n<td>an area or a surface: <em>on the terrace</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>in</strong> (place)</td>\n<td>a place with a boundary: <em>in a room; in the sea</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>towards, into, up, in, around, across</strong> (direction)</td>\n<td>a direction: <em>towards the church; across the street</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>In</em> is also used when something is contained in a bigger thing: <em>in the box, in the house</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></p>\n<p>The preposition of <strong>agent</strong> (the doer) is <em>by</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><em>By</em> is used to …</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>show how something has been done</td>\n<td>manufactured <strong>by</strong> machines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>show how somebody commutes (travels)</td>\n<td>travels <strong>by</strong> bus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>show the agent in a passive sentence</td>\n<td>We were informed <strong>by</strong> the authorities.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>show work written or done by people</td>\n<td>built <strong>by</strong> a carpenter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>passive</strong> sentence puts the receiver of the action first (<em>We were informed</em>); the doer, if we mention it, comes after <em>by</em>.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">At, on, in: from a point to a bigger space</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Time: <strong>at</strong> a point (<em>at noon</em>), <strong>on</strong> a day (<em>on Wednesday</em>), <strong>in</strong> a longer period (<em>in January, in 2017</em>). Place: <strong>at</strong> a point (<em>at the library</em>), <strong>on</strong> a surface (<em>on the terrace</em>), <strong>in</strong> a space with a boundary (<em>in a room</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"thirty-two-common-prepositions\">Thirty-two common prepositions</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s table of commonly used prepositions: each meaning (in italics) is followed by the slide’s example. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Preposition</th>\n<th>Meaning: the slide’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>about</td>\n<td>(i) <em>on the subject of</em>: This movie is <strong>about</strong> three students in a college. (ii) <em>approximately</em>: Take <strong>about</strong> five grams of copper dioxide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>across</td>\n<td><em>from one side to the other</em>: We walked <strong>across</strong> the road to the ice-cream parlour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>against</td>\n<td>(i) <em>opposed to</em>: It is <strong>against</strong> the law to obstruct government officials while they are performing their duty. (ii) <em>in contact with</em>: Place the refrigerator <strong>against</strong> the wall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after</td>\n<td>(i) <em>later in time</em>: Please meet me <strong>after</strong> 5 o’clock. (ii) <em>later in a group</em>: The dancers will come <strong>after</strong> singers in the procession. (iii) <em>following somebody or something</em>: The policemen ran <strong>after</strong> the thieves.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>along</td>\n<td><em>following</em>: Walk <strong>along</strong> the road till you see the bus-stop on the right.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>at</td>\n<td>(i) <em>a specific location</em>: Please deliver this parcel <strong>at</strong> 12, Trundle Street. (ii) <em>a point of time</em>: The instructor specifically told us to submit the assignment <strong>at</strong> 9.00 pm. (iii) <em>an activity</em>: The students are busy <strong>at</strong> work. (iv) <em>towards somebody or something</em>: The bull charged <strong>at</strong> the crowd.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>before</td>\n<td>(i) <em>earlier in time</em>: Deliver this parcel <strong>before</strong> 9 pm. (ii) <em>earlier in a group of things</em>: R comes <strong>before</strong> S in the alphabet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>between</td>\n<td>(i) <em>in terms of location</em>: The station lies <strong>between</strong> the mountain and the river. (ii) <em>in terms of time</em>: I will try and visit you <strong>between</strong> Christmas and the New Year. (iii) <em>with reference to two things</em>: The property was divided equally <strong>between</strong> the two brothers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>but</td>\n<td><em>except</em>: I have packed everything <strong>but</strong> the food.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>by</td>\n<td>(i) <em>near</em>: I live <strong>by</strong> the seashore. (ii) <em>within a certain time</em>: Try to finish this project <strong>by</strong> next week. (iii) <em>through</em>: We are travelling <strong>by</strong> plane to Delhi.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>down</td>\n<td>(i) <em>towards a lower position</em>: Jack rolled <strong>down</strong> the hill. (ii) <em>further along</em>: Raghu lives <strong>down</strong> this road.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>during</td>\n<td>(i) <em>throughout a period</em>: BPO staff work <strong>during</strong> the night. (ii) <em>sometime in the course of the period</em>: It rained heavily sometime <strong>during</strong> the night.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>for</td>\n<td>(i) <em>duration of time</em>: We played football <strong>for</strong> two hours. (ii) <em>distance</em>: You have to walk <strong>for</strong> two kilometers to reach the bank. (iii) <em>purpose</em>: These bananas are <strong>for</strong> the custard.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>from</td>\n<td>(i) <em>place of origin</em>: We started <strong>from</strong> Varanasi yesterday. (ii) <em>source</em>: We heard about the accident <strong>from</strong> Rohan. (iii) <em>cause</em>: She suffers <strong>from</strong> migraine. That’s why she gets frequent headaches.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in</td>\n<td>(i) <em>place</em>: London is <strong>in</strong> England. (ii) <em>within a place</em>: I left my bag <strong>in</strong> the room. (iii) <em>a member of</em>: He is <strong>in</strong> the army.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inside</td>\n<td><em>within</em>: They are <strong>inside</strong> the room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>into</td>\n<td><em>to the inside of</em>: We stepped <strong>into</strong> the hall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>like</td>\n<td>(i) <em>resembling</em>: He looks <strong>like</strong> a movie actor. (ii) <em>possibility</em>: It appears <strong>like</strong> rain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>near</td>\n<td><em>close to</em>: The park is <strong>near</strong> the school.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>of</td>\n<td>(i) <em>location</em>: Don’t stand in the middle <strong>of</strong> the road. (ii) <em>possession</em>: Rohan is a good friend <strong>of</strong> mine. (iii) <em>part of a group</em>: Vikrant is a part <strong>of</strong> the music band. (iv) <em>measurement</em>: Take two cups <strong>of</strong> milk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>off</td>\n<td>(i) <em>not on</em>: Please keep <strong>off</strong> the grass. (ii) <em>at some distance from</em>: The island is <strong>off</strong> the coast.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>onto</td>\n<td><em>reaching the position</em>: The dog climbed <strong>onto</strong> the table.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>over</td>\n<td>(i) <em>above</em>: There are shelves <strong>over</strong> the sink. (ii) <em>across</em>: He tried to jump <strong>over</strong> the wall. (iii) <em>more than</em>: This phone is <strong>over</strong> two thousand rupees.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>since</td>\n<td>(i) <em>from a specific time in the past</em>: I have been working on this assignment <strong>since</strong> 2 pm. (ii) <em>from a past time until now</em>: I have been waiting for the courier <strong>since</strong> morning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>through</td>\n<td>(i) <em>for a period of time</em>: I slept <strong>through</strong> the night. (ii) <em>by means of</em>: He started drawing well <strong>through</strong> practice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to</td>\n<td>(i) <em>in the direction of</em>: Turn <strong>to</strong> your right. (ii) <em>destination</em>: I am going <strong>to</strong> Varanasi. (iii) <em>purpose</em>: I went shopping <strong>to</strong> buy new clothes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>toward(s)</td>\n<td>(i) <em>in the direction of</em>: She started walking <strong>towards</strong> the temple. (ii) <em>just before the time</em>: It started raining <strong>towards</strong> night.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>under</td>\n<td>(i) <em>beneath</em>: The books are <strong>under</strong> the table. (ii) <em>in the present condition</em>: My car is <strong>under</strong> repair.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>until</td>\n<td><em>up to a certain point of time</em>: She will stay <strong>until</strong> 4 pm.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>up</td>\n<td>(i) <em>to a higher place</em>: We walked <strong>up</strong> the stairs. (ii) <em>in a higher place</em>: The temple is <strong>up</strong> the hill.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>with</td>\n<td>(i) <em>accompanying</em>: I went to the park <strong>with</strong> my parents. (ii) <em>by means of</em>: He stuck the paper <strong>with</strong> glue. (iii) <em>as a result of</em>: My mother was very happy <strong>with</strong> my performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>within</td>\n<td>(i) <em>inside of, in terms of distance</em>: My college is <strong>within</strong> one kilometer from here. (ii) <em>before the end of a time period</em>: I will come back <strong>within</strong> an hour.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The table gives approximate meanings; these prepositions can be used in more contexts than these. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>To</em> before a verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>I went shopping <strong>to buy</strong> new clothes</em>, <em>to</em> comes before a verb (<em>buy</em>). Grammar books call this <em>to</em> the <strong>infinitive marker</strong> (<em>to</em> + V1), not a preposition. As a preposition, <em>to</em> is followed by a noun or a pronoun: <em>to your right, to Varanasi</em>. (Note added here.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How the lecture asks you to practise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Write <strong>your own sentence for each meaning</strong> in the table, about 60 sentences, <strong>with a pen on paper</strong> in a notebook, not typed on a machine. Take your time and stay relaxed.</li>\n<li>Write an <strong>equal number</strong> of sentences in your own language (the same sentences or new ones), and notice how its postpositions are used.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Rest will happen in your subconscious”, and it will show when you speak and write. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajGNF1CO_Vs&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"conjunctions\">Conjunctions</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>conjunction</strong> is a joiner. Like a bridge between two banks, it connects two pieces of language. Other names for conjunctions are <strong>connectors</strong> and <strong>linkers</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conjunction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Conjunctions</strong> are joiners. They are used to join <strong>words</strong>, <strong>phrases</strong> or <strong>clauses</strong>, so they can even join two sentences into one. <em>And</em>, <em>but</em> and <em>or</em> are three common conjunctions. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>It joins</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n<th>The parts joined</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>words</td>\n<td>Jack <strong>and</strong> Jill went up the hill.</td>\n<td><em>Jack</em>, <em>Jill</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>phrases</td>\n<td>Toby is a highly intelligent <strong>but</strong> sometimes quiet boy.</td>\n<td><em>highly intelligent</em>, <em>sometimes quiet</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clauses</td>\n<td>You can prepare the salad, <strong>or</strong> you can clean the floor.</td>\n<td><em>You can prepare the salad</em>, <em>you can clean the floor</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>These pairings are only examples: <em>and, but, or</em> (like most conjunctions) can join words, phrases or clauses. <em>You can prepare the salad <strong>and</strong> you can clean the floor</em> is also correct. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></p>\n<p>There are three types: <strong>coordinating</strong>, <strong>subordinating</strong> and <strong>correlative</strong> conjunctions. The basic difference to remember is the one between coordinating and subordinating. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"coordinating-conjunctions\">Coordinating conjunctions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Coordinating conjunction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>coordinating conjunction</strong> joins <strong>like with like</strong>: a noun with another noun, an adjective with another adjective, a clause with another clause. The two parts have <strong>equal status</strong>: neither depends on the other. There are seven: <strong>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">FANBOYS</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>F</strong>or, <strong>A</strong>nd, <strong>N</strong>or, <strong>B</strong>ut, <strong>O</strong>r, <strong>Y</strong>et, <strong>S</strong>o: the first letters spell <strong>FANBOYS</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Conjunction</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>and</td>\n<td>in addition</td>\n<td>She tried <strong>and</strong> succeeded.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>but</td>\n<td>however</td>\n<td>They tried <strong>but</strong> did not succeed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>or</td>\n<td>alternatively</td>\n<td>Did you go out <strong>or</strong> stay at home?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nor</td>\n<td>and neither</td>\n<td>I did not see it, <strong>nor</strong> did they.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yet</td>\n<td>however</td>\n<td>The sun is warm, <strong>yet</strong> the air is cool.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>But</em> and <em>yet</em>, like <em>however</em>, show <strong>contrast</strong>: the second idea goes against what the first one leads you to expect. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><em>Or</em> gives <strong>alternatives</strong> (choices). <em>Nor</em> is the <strong>negative</strong>: it joins a choice that is also denied. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n<li><em>For</em> (meaning <em>because</em>) and <em>so</em> (meaning <em>consequently</em>) are the other two; the slides give their examples in the subordinating table below.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"subordinating-conjunctions\">Subordinating conjunctions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Main clause and subordinate clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>main clause</strong> (independent clause) makes complete sense on its own: <em>I will help him.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>subordinate clause</strong> (dependent clause) needs the main clause to make complete sense: <em>As he is my friend …</em> (then what?).</li>\n<li>A sentence with both is called a <strong>complex sentence</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Subordinating conjunction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>subordinating conjunction</strong> joins a <strong>subordinate clause</strong> to a <strong>main clause</strong>. Like a coordinating conjunction, it connects; but here the two parts are <strong>not equal</strong>: one depends on the other. Common examples: <em>although, because, if, since, unless, until, while</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the two clauses (the lecture’s sentence)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>As he is my friend, I will help him.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the conjunction: <strong>as</strong>, meaning <em>because</em>.</li>\n<li>The clause it begins, <em>as he is my friend</em>, cannot stand alone: it is the <strong>subordinate clause</strong>.</li>\n<li>The rest, <em>I will help him</em>, makes complete sense: it is the <strong>main clause</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> main clause <em>I will help him</em>; subordinate clause <em>as he is my friend</em>, joined by the subordinating conjunction <em>as</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s table: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Conjunction</th>\n<th>Meaning: the slide’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>as</td>\n<td>(i) <em>because</em>: <strong>As</strong> he is my friend, I will help him. (ii) <em>when</em>: We watched <strong>as</strong> the plane took off.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after</td>\n<td><em>later in time</em>: <strong>After</strong> the train left, we went home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>although, though</td>\n<td><em>despite</em>: <strong>Although</strong> it was after midnight, we did not feel tired.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>before</td>\n<td><em>earlier than</em>: I arrived <strong>before</strong> the stores were open.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>because</td>\n<td><em>for the reason that</em>: We had to wait, <strong>because</strong> we arrived early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>for</td>\n<td><em>because</em>: He is happy, <strong>for</strong> he enjoys his work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>if</td>\n<td><em>on condition that</em>: <strong>If</strong> she is here, we will see her.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lest</td>\n<td><em>for fear that</em>: I watched closely, <strong>lest</strong> he make a mistake.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>providing, provided</td>\n<td><em>on condition that</em>: All will be well, <strong>providing</strong> you are careful.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>since</td>\n<td>(i) <em>from a past time</em>: I have been here <strong>since</strong> the sun rose. (ii) <em>as, because</em>: <strong>Since</strong> you are here, you can help me.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>so, so that</td>\n<td>(i) <em>consequently</em>: It was raining, <strong>so</strong> we did not go out. (ii) <em>in order that</em>: I am saving money <strong>so</strong> I can buy a bicycle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>supposing</td>\n<td><em>if</em>: <strong>Supposing</strong> that happens, what will you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>than</td>\n<td><em>used in comparisons</em>: He is taller <strong>than</strong> you are.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unless</td>\n<td><em>except when, if not</em>: <strong>Unless</strong> he helps us, we cannot succeed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>until, till</td>\n<td><em>up to the time when</em>: I will wait <strong>until</strong> I hear from you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>whereas</td>\n<td>(i) <em>because</em>: <strong>Whereas</strong> this is a public building, it is open to everyone. (ii) <em>on the other hand</em>: He is short, <strong>whereas</strong> you are tall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>whether</td>\n<td><em>if</em>: I do not know <strong>whether</strong> she was invited.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>while</td>\n<td>(i) <em>at the time when</em>: <strong>While</strong> it was snowing, we played cards. (ii) <em>on the other hand</em>: He is rich, <strong>while</strong> his friend is poor. (iii) <em>although</em>: <strong>While</strong> I am not an expert, I will do my best.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>So</em> or <em>so that</em>?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When <em>so</em> means <strong>in order that</strong>, formal writing adds <em>that</em>: <em>I am saving money <strong>so that</strong> I can buy a bicycle.</em> In speech, <em>so</em> alone is common. When <em>so</em> means <strong>consequently</strong> (<em>It was raining, so we did not go out</em>), no <em>that</em> is needed. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">After <em>lest</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide underlines <em>make</em> in <em>lest he make a mistake</em>: after <em>lest</em>, the verb stays in its base form V1, even after <em>he</em>. (Explanation added here.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Some <strong>phrases</strong> also begin subordinate clauses: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Meaning: the slide’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>as if</td>\n<td><em>in a similar way</em>: She talks <strong>as if</strong> she knows everything.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as long as</td>\n<td>(i) <em>if</em>: <strong>As long as</strong> we cooperate, we can finish the work easily. (ii) <em>while</em>: He has lived there <strong>as long as</strong> I have known him.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>as soon as</td>\n<td><em>immediately when</em>: Write to me <strong>as soon as</strong> you can.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>even if</td>\n<td><em>in spite of a possibility</em>: I am going out <strong>even if</strong> it rains.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"correlative-conjunctions\">Correlative conjunctions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Correlative conjunction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Correlative conjunctions</strong> are used <strong>in pairs</strong> to join alternatives or equal elements. They work like coordinating conjunctions: the two parts have equal status, and neither depends on the other. The most common pairs are <em>either … or</em>, <em>neither … nor</em> and <em>not only … but also</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pair</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>both … and</td>\n<td>He is <strong>both</strong> intelligent <strong>and</strong> good-natured.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>either … or</td>\n<td>I will <strong>either</strong> go for a walk <strong>or</strong> read a book.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>neither … nor</td>\n<td>He is <strong>neither</strong> rich <strong>nor</strong> famous.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hardly … when</td>\n<td>He had <strong>hardly</strong> begun to work, <strong>when</strong> he was interrupted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>if … then</td>\n<td><strong>If</strong> that is true, <strong>then</strong> what happened is not surprising.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no sooner … than</td>\n<td><strong>No sooner</strong> had I reached the corner, <strong>than</strong> the bus came.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not only … but also</td>\n<td>She is <strong>not only</strong> clever, <strong>but also</strong> hard-working.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rather … than</td>\n<td>I would <strong>rather</strong> go swimming <strong>than</strong> go to the library.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scarcely … when</td>\n<td><strong>Scarcely</strong> had we left home, <strong>when</strong> it started to rain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>what with … and</td>\n<td><strong>What with</strong> all her aunts, uncles <strong>and</strong> cousins, she has many relatives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>whether … or</td>\n<td>Have you decided <strong>whether</strong> you will come <strong>or</strong> not?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">One word, more than one part of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many English words belong to more than one class. <em>Since</em> is a <strong>preposition</strong> in <em>since 2 pm</em> (Lecture 2.3’s table), a <strong>conjunction</strong> in <em>Since you are here, you can help me</em>, and it can also be an <strong>adverb</strong>. <em>For</em> is a preposition in <em>for two hours</em> and a conjunction in <em>He is happy, for he enjoys his work</em>. So do not memorise a word as “a conjunction” or “a preposition”. Look at its <strong>function</strong> in the sentence: does it join two clauses, stand before a noun, or tell us about the verb? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"interjections\">Interjections</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Comics and cartoons are full of words like <em>yikes, aha, boom</em>. In daily talk we also use short words to show a feeling at once: <em>wow, ouch, oops</em>. These words are <strong>interjections</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interjection</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Interjections</strong> are words that have no real meaning of their own, but help us show a <strong>sudden emotion</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are words or short phrases that are <strong>independent</strong> of the rest of the sentence.</li>\n<li>They are <strong>not marked</strong> for tense, number (singular or plural), aspect or any other grammatical feature.</li>\n<li>They are more common in <strong>speech</strong> than in writing.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interjection</th>\n<th>Emotion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wow</td>\n<td>amazement, surprise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Argh</td>\n<td>frustration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ouch</td>\n<td>pain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oops</td>\n<td>regret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hi, Hey</td>\n<td>salutation: to start (or end) a conversation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecturer also writes <em>aww, aw, eh, huh, nah, boom, kaboom</em>. Instead of <em>wow</em>, people also say <em>marvellous</em>, <em>amazing</em> or <em>awesome</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not for formal writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Interjections make speech sound real and natural, but they are <strong>not suitable for writing</strong>. Grammatically they stand alone: they depend on no other word in the sentence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxCVjgMfW34&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"articles-a-and-an\">Articles: a and an</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Before a noun, English often adds a tiny word that tells the listener <strong>which one</strong> we mean. <em>A</em> or <em>an</em> means “one, any one, not known yet”. <em>The</em> means “this particular one, you know which”. These three words are <strong>articles</strong>. Native speakers learn them by intuition as children; adults can reach the same accuracy by paying attention to contexts and a few rules. Using articles correctly makes your English impressive. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Article</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Articles</strong> are the three words <strong>a</strong>, <strong>an</strong> and <strong>the</strong>. Grammatically they are also called <strong>determiners</strong>, and they are a <strong>closed class</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indefinite articles</strong>: <em>a, an</em>, for an indefinite context (any one, not identified).</li>\n<li><strong>Definite article</strong>: <em>the</em>, for a definite context (a particular, identified one).</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: articles come <strong>only in front of a noun</strong>. Turn it around: if you see an article, a noun follows. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Our languages do the same job differently</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, Bangla, Marathi and many other Indian languages have no word like <em>a, an, the</em>. The job is not missing: these languages show definite and indefinite in other ways. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Where <em>a</em> occurs, <em>an</em> does not occur.</strong> The choice depends on the <strong>first sound</strong> of the next word, not on its spelling: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a</strong> in front of a <strong>consonant sound</strong>: <em>a cat</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>an</strong> in front of a <strong>vowel sound</strong>: <em>an honest man</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why “an”?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look at what the two forms are made of. <em>A</em> is one <strong>vowel</strong> sound (V). <em>An</em> is a <strong>vowel + a consonant</strong>, <em>n</em> (V C). English likes consonants and vowels to alternate. Before a consonant sound, <em>a</em> gives V C: <em>a cat</em>. Before a vowel sound, <em>a</em> would put two vowels side by side, so we use <em>an</em>, and its <em>n</em> comes in between: <em>an honest</em> gives V C V. So there is really <strong>one</strong> indefinite article with two forms, and the form makes speaking easier. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The 44 sounds of English (from <a href=\"#/english1/w01\">Week 1</a>), as on the slide: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Sounds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>9 voiceless consonants</td>\n<td>p, t, k, tʃ, f, θ, s, ʃ, h</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 voiced consonants</td>\n<td>b, d, g, dʒ, v, ð, z, ʒ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 other consonants</td>\n<td>m, n, ŋ (nasals); l, r (liquids); w, j (semi-vowels)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 long vowels</td>\n<td>iː, ɑː, ɔː, uː, ɜː</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 short vowels</td>\n<td>ɪ, e, æ, ʌ, ɒ, ʊ, ə</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 diphthongs</td>\n<td>not shown on the slide</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So 24 consonant sounds and 20 vowel sounds make 44. A word that begins with any of the consonant sounds takes <strong>a</strong>. A word that begins with a vowel sound, even a diphthong (<em>an owl</em>, <em>an Einstein</em>), takes <strong>an</strong>. (Here <em>j</em> is the sound of <em>y</em> in <em>yes</em>.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an? (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word: its first sound</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>cat</em>: /k/, a consonant → <strong>a</strong></td>\n<td><strong>A</strong> cat sat on the table.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>honest</em>: /ɒ/, a vowel (the <em>h</em> is silent) → <strong>an</strong></td>\n<td>He is <strong>an</strong> honest man.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>MA</em>: /e/ of “em”, a vowel → <strong>an</strong></td>\n<td>I have <strong>an</strong> MA in English studies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>European</em>: /j/, the <em>y</em> sound of <em>you</em>, a consonant → <strong>a</strong></td>\n<td>It was <strong>a</strong> wonderful European trip.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>one</em>: /w/, a consonant → <strong>a</strong></td>\n<td>It was <strong>a</strong> one to one match.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Go by the sound, not the letter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The spelling can mislead you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>H</em> is silent in <em>honest</em> and <em>hour</em>: <strong>an</strong> honest man, <strong>an</strong> hour.</li>\n<li><em>M</em> is a consonant letter, but its name “em” starts with a vowel sound: <strong>an</strong> MA.</li>\n<li><em>E</em> and <em>o</em> are vowel letters, but <em>European</em> starts with /j/ and <em>one</em> with /w/: <strong>a</strong> European trip, <strong>a</strong> one to one match. <em>W</em> and <em>y</em> are called <strong>semi-vowels</strong>, but they are consonant sounds. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The next word decides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>A</em> or <em>an</em> depends on the word <strong>right after</strong> it, even when that word is an adjective: <em>She drives <strong>an</strong> amazing car</em>, but <em>She drives <strong>a</strong> big car</em> (Lecture 2.2); <em>They caught <strong>a</strong> big fish</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"five-uses-of-a-and-an\">Five uses of a and an</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s list: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Expressions of quantity and number</td>\n<td>I ate <strong>a</strong> lot of laddus. I waited <strong>an</strong> hour for him. She spent <strong>a</strong> couple of days in Guntur. They bought <strong>a</strong> number of balloons.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Before a proper noun that is not significant</td>\n<td><strong>A</strong> Mr. Nikhil came to see you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Before a proper noun used figuratively</td>\n<td>He wanted to be <strong>an</strong> Einstein. She is <strong>a</strong> Hitler.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Before a singular class noun, to mean the whole class</td>\n<td><strong>An</strong> owl can see in darkness. <strong>A</strong> camel can live without water for many days.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Before a singular class noun mentioned for the first time, not known to the listener or reader</td>\n<td>I saw <strong>a</strong> bird. They caught <strong>a</strong> big fish. He left this place <strong>an</strong> hour ago.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In use 2, <em>a Mr. Nikhil</em> means “someone called Mr. Nikhil”: the speaker does not present him as anyone important. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></li>\n<li>In use 3, <em>an Einstein</em> is not the man himself: it means a genius like Einstein. This is a <strong>figure of speech</strong> (a word used in a non-literal way). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n<li>In use 4, <em>an owl</em> means owls in general: we talk about what every owl can do, not about one particular owl. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">How far five uses take you</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer: keep just these five uses in mind and you cover about 60 to 70 per cent of the uses of the indefinite article in regular speech and writing. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-definite-article\">The definite article</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>The</em> points to a <strong>particular</strong> thing that the listener can identify: because it was mentioned before, because there is only one, or because the sentence tells us exactly which one. Its written form never changes: <em>the</em> stands before consonant sounds and vowel sounds alike (<em>the cat, the owl</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXWalyM03b8&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">How <em>the</em> sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In speech, many speakers say <em>the</em> as “thee” before a vowel sound (<em>the owl</em>) and “thuh” before a consonant sound (<em>the cat</em>). The spelling stays the same. (Note added here.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">First mention, then second mention (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>I saw <strong>a</strong> snake.</em> The listener knows nothing about this snake yet, so we use the indefinite article <em>a</em>.</li>\n<li><em>The snake was a cobra.</em> Now the listener knows which snake: the one I saw. So we use the definite article <em>the</em>.</li>\n<li>The same idea: <em>You gave me a book. <strong>The</strong> book is missing.</em> In one sentence: <em>The book you gave me is missing.</em> The words <em>you gave me</em> say exactly which book. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>a</em> for the first mention; <em>the</em> once the listener knows which one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s uses of <em>the</em>: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use of <em>the</em></th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. A class noun already known to the listener or the reader</td>\n<td>I saw a snake. <strong>The</strong> snake was a cobra. <strong>The</strong> book you gave me is missing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. A class noun for the whole class (<em>a/an</em> can also be used)</td>\n<td><strong>The</strong> crow is black (also: <em>A crow is black</em>). <strong>The</strong> elephant has a trunk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. A proper noun in a figurative sense</td>\n<td>Kalidas is <strong>the</strong> Shakespeare of India.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. A surname for more than one person</td>\n<td><strong>The</strong> Chettiars live next to my house. <strong>The</strong> Smiths are coming.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Nouns that are only one (unique reference)</td>\n<td><strong>The</strong> sun rises in <strong>the</strong> east. <strong>The</strong> prime minister is arriving today. Also: the moon, the earth, the president, the Milky Way.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. A material noun in a specific sense</td>\n<td><strong>the</strong> water of the Ganga; <strong>the</strong> rice of Dehradun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. Names of rivers, lakes, seas, oceans, groups of islands, mountain ranges, newspapers, ships and some classical books</td>\n<td>the Nile, the Ganga, the Chilka lake, the Arabian Sea, the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, the Himalayas, The Times of India, The Hindu, the Bible, the Mahabharat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8. A noun followed by the preposition <em>of</em></td>\n<td><strong>the</strong> front of my house; <strong>the</strong> pages of my diary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9. Before an adjective in the superlative degree</td>\n<td>She is <strong>the</strong> brightest girl in the class. This is one of <strong>the</strong> most interesting books I have ever read.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10. With the comparative degree, when an activity increases or decreases</td>\n<td><strong>The</strong> more he received, <strong>the</strong> more he wanted. <strong>The</strong> sooner you start, <strong>the</strong> better for you.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Use 3: <em>the Shakespeare of India</em> means that Kalidas is for India what Shakespeare is for England. We are not talking about Shakespeare himself. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></li>\n<li>Use 4: <em>the Chettiars</em> means all the people of the Chettiar family, not one person. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></li>\n<li>Use 6: we mean not water in general but the water of the Ganga: the material is <strong>specified</strong> and separated from the rest. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Degrees of comparison</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An adjective has a plain form (<em>bright</em>), a <strong>comparative</strong> form for comparing two (<em>brighter</em>), and a <strong>superlative</strong> form for the top of a group (<em>brightest</em>, <em>most interesting</em>). A superlative points to one unique member of the group, so it takes <em>the</em>: <em>the brightest girl</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Exception to use 8: a noun in apposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A noun is <strong>in apposition</strong> when it stands right next to another noun to rename it, usually between commas. Such a noun takes no <em>the</em>, even when <em>of</em> follows: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arundhati Roy, <strong>author of</strong> <em>The God of Small Things</em>, has become a political activist.</li>\n<li>Mr. Arjun, <strong>owner of</strong> this house, is my friend.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s reason: here <em>author of …</em> works like a name for the person, so the <em>of</em> rule does not apply.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Read with attention</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When you read, notice the nouns that have an article in front of them, and ask <strong>why</strong> that article is there. Close attention to such contexts builds your accuracy and your confidence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-zero-article\">The zero article</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Zero article</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A noun does not always need one of the three articles. When a noun has <strong>no article</strong>, we call it the <strong>zero article</strong> (in that sense, a fourth article). The lecture gives two contexts. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Context</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Class nouns that are part of the <strong>daily routine</strong>: <em>home, church, temple, hospital, masjid, prison, work, sea, town, market, railway station, bus, train, bed, court, college, university, office, father and mother</em></td>\n<td>He is going <strong>home</strong>. I go to <strong>church</strong> every Sunday. She was admitted to <strong>hospital</strong> yesterday. <strong>Father</strong> is out of station. <strong>Mother</strong> is in the kitchen. He was confined to <strong>bed</strong> for three days.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. <strong>Material</strong> and <strong>abstract</strong> nouns, unless they are specified</td>\n<td>He bought five bars of <strong>gold</strong>. The small child needs <strong>care</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not “a home”, not “the home”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>We do not say ✗ <em>He is going a home</em> or ✗ <em>He is going to the home</em>; we say <em>He is going home</em>. Going to church every Sunday is part of my routine, so we say <em>I go to church every Sunday</em>, not ✗ <em>I go to the church every Sunday</em> or ✗ <em>I go to a church every Sunday</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-1ZDPS6EC0&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Specified or general</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Gold</em> in general takes no article (<em>five bars of gold</em>), but a specified material takes <em>the</em> (<em>the water of the Ganga</em>, use 6 above). In <em>Mother is in <strong>the</strong> kitchen</em>, <em>Mother</em> has no article (it is used like a name in the family), while <em>kitchen</em> is not on the routine list and means our particular kitchen, so it takes <em>the</em>. (Explanation added here.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Choosing an article, step by step</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A way to remember the lectures’ rules:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the noun a daily-routine place (<em>home, church, hospital, bed</em>), or a material or abstract noun used in general (<em>gold, care</em>)? → <strong>no article</strong>.</li>\n<li>Can the listener tell exactly which one: mentioned before, the only one, specified (<em>of the Ganga</em>, <em>you gave me</em>), a superlative, a river, a newspaper, a classic? → <strong>the</strong>.</li>\n<li>Otherwise, for a singular countable noun (one, any one, a first mention, or the whole class) → <strong>a</strong> or <strong>an</strong>, chosen by the first sound of the next word.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part of speech</strong>: the name of a word by its job in a sentence. Course list: noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, article, conjunction; plus interjection.</li>\n<li><strong>Verb</strong>: action or state; every sentence has one; only verbs carry tense. <em>He/she/it</em> + V1 + s; <em>I/you/we/they</em> + V1; <em>I am, he is, you are, they are</em>. Auxiliaries (<em>be, do, have, can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>) help the main verb. Only one verb agrees with the subject; <em>to go for a walk</em> is a verbal noun.</li>\n<li><strong>Noun</strong>: naming word. Countable: proper (<em>India</em>), class (<em>cat</em>), collective (<em>jury</em>). Uncountable: abstract (<em>goodness</em>), material (<em>gold</em>). Signs: it takes a plural; it follows an article or a preposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Pronoun</strong>: replaces a noun. Subject: <em>I, you, he, she, it, we, they</em>. Object: <em>me, you, him, her, it, us, them</em>. <em>You</em> always takes the plural verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjective</strong>: describes a noun or pronoun. Before the noun: attributive (the lecture’s “distributive”); after the verb: predicative. Order: opinion, size, colour, type.</li>\n<li><strong>Adverb</strong>: tells about a verb, an adjective or an adverb; answers when, where, how, why, in what way. Phrases can work as adverbs (<em>from Delhi</em>). <em>-ly</em> is not the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong> = doer (who?); <strong>object</strong> = receives the action (what?); <strong>predicate</strong> = everything except the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Preposition</strong>: closed class; time, place, position; before a noun or pronoun; preposition + noun = prepositional phrase. Time: <em>at</em> a point, <em>on</em> a day or date, <em>in</em> a longer period, <em>for</em> a duration. Place: <em>at</em> a point, <em>on</em> a surface, <em>in</em> a boundary. <em>By</em>: means, travel, the agent. Indian languages use postpositions.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjunctions</strong>: coordinating (FANBOYS; equal parts); subordinating (subordinate clause + main clause: <em>as, because, although, if, unless, while …</em>, and phrases <em>as if, as long as, as soon as, even if</em>); correlative (pairs: <em>both … and, either … or, neither … nor, not only … but also …</em>). Formal writing: <em>so that</em> = in order that.</li>\n<li><strong>Interjection</strong>: sudden emotion (<em>wow, argh, ouch, oops, hi</em>); independent; no tense or number; for speech, not formal writing.</li>\n<li><strong>A/an</strong>: by the first <strong>sound</strong> of the next word: <em>a cat, a European, a one to one match</em>; <em>an honest man, an hour, an MA, an owl</em>. Uses: quantity (<em>a lot of</em>), an unimportant name (<em>a Mr. Nikhil</em>), figurative (<em>an Einstein</em>), whole class (<em>an owl</em>), first mention (<em>a bird</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>The</strong>: known or second mention, whole class, figurative (<em>the Shakespeare of India</em>), families (<em>the Smiths</em>), unique (<em>the sun</em>), specified material (<em>the water of the Ganga</em>), rivers, seas, mountains, newspapers, classics, noun + <em>of</em> (not in apposition), superlatives, <em>the more … the more</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero article</strong>: daily-routine places (<em>go home, go to church, admitted to hospital</em>); material and abstract nouns in general (<em>gold, care</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In <em>She runs very fast</em>, what part of speech is <em>very</em>, and which word does it modify?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Adverb.</strong> <em>Very</em> answers “how fast?”, so it gives information about <em>fast</em>. <em>Fast</em> is itself an adverb: it tells how she runs. So <em>very</em> is an adverb that modifies another adverb (the lecture’s adverb exercise).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>from Delhi</em> in <em>I came from Delhi</em> an adjective?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>No.</strong> An adjective describes a noun or a pronoun, and <em>from Delhi</em> describes no noun. It tells <strong>where</strong> I came from, so the whole phrase works as an <strong>adverb</strong> of the verb <em>came</em>. Inside it, <em>from</em> is a preposition and <em>Delhi</em> is a proper noun.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Choose the verb: <em>Your dogs (likes / like) to go for a walk.</em> How many verbs agree with the subject?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Like.</strong> The subject <em>dogs</em> is plural, so the verb takes V1 without <em>-s</em>. Only one verb, <em>like</em>, agrees with the subject; <em>to go for a walk</em> is a verbal noun (it answers “like what?”).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>A</em> or <em>an</em>? ___ hour, ___ European country, ___ MA, ___ one to one match, ___ unanimous decision</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>an</strong> hour: the <em>h</em> is silent, so the first sound is a vowel.</li>\n<li><strong>a</strong> European country: the first sound is /j/ (the <em>y</em> sound), a consonant.</li>\n<li><strong>an</strong> MA: we say “em”, which starts with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><strong>a</strong> one to one match: the first sound is /w/, a consonant.</li>\n<li><strong>a</strong> unanimous decision: <em>unanimous</em> starts with /j/ (“you-”), a consonant sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rule looks at the sound, not the letter.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Coordinating, subordinating or correlative? (i) <em>She tried and succeeded.</em> (ii) <em>Although it was after midnight, we did not feel tired.</em> (iii) <em>He is neither rich nor famous.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(i) <strong>Coordinating</strong> (<em>and</em>): <em>tried</em> and <em>succeeded</em> are equal; neither depends on the other.</li>\n<li>(ii) <strong>Subordinating</strong> (<em>although</em> = despite): <em>although it was after midnight</em> cannot stand alone; it depends on the main clause <em>we did not feel tired</em>.</li>\n<li>(iii) <strong>Correlative</strong> (<em>neither … nor</em>): a pair that joins two equal elements, <em>rich</em> and <em>famous</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s exercise (Lecture 2.6): fill in <em>a, an</em> or <em>the</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>___ seeds you sowed last week have sprung up.</td>\n<td>The</td>\n<td>specified: the seeds <em>you sowed last week</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>___ bird was flying leisurely in the sky.</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>first mention, not known to the listener</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is such ___ difficult task that only ___ few are capable of doing it.</td>\n<td>a, a</td>\n<td>one task, not identified; <em>a few</em> is an expression of quantity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Look at ___ sky; there are ___ sun and ___ moon shining together.</td>\n<td>the, the, the</td>\n<td>unique reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He had ___ lot of money.</td>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>expression of quantity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You can’t deny ___ fact that this revolver is yours.</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td>specified by <em>that this revolver is yours</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Are you going to see ___ world cup match in West Indies?</td>\n<td>the</td>\n<td>one particular match that both people know</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will you send ___ mechanic to repair my car?</td>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>any mechanic, not a particular one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They have left me in ___ unsettling phase.</td>\n<td>an</td>\n<td><em>unsettling</em> starts with the vowel sound /ʌ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>___ prime minister asked his ministers to arrive at ___ unanimous decision.</td>\n<td>The, a</td>\n<td>a unique post; <em>unanimous</em> starts with /j/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture leaves this exercise for you to work out; these answers and reasons follow its rules.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
